-- ===== Module 1: How Code Actually Runs =====
INSERT INTO pm_learning_modules (course_id, title, description, sort_order)
SELECT @course, 'How Code Actually Runs', 'What programming languages are, how a computer turns written code into action, how a browser turns HTML into a living page, and the different ways developers think about organising code.', 1 FROM DUAL WHERE @course > 0;
SET @mod := IF(@course > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);

INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Programming languages: compiled, interpreted and why it matters', 'Why there are so many languages, how code becomes something a computer can run, and what that means for the Repairs project.', '## Computers only understand numbers\n\nAt the very bottom, a computer understands nothing but long strings of on and off signals (**machine code**). Nobody writes software that way. Instead, developers write in a **programming language** that is readable to humans, and a tool translates it for the machine.\n\nThere are hundreds of languages for the same reason there are many kinds of vehicle: different jobs suit different designs. A few you will hear about:\n\n- **JavaScript** runs in every browser. It makes web pages interactive, and (via Node.js) can also run on servers.\n- **Python** is loved for readability, data analysis, automation and AI.\n- **PHP** powers a large share of the web, including WordPress and this very learning site.\n- **Java** and **C#** are staples of large organisations and many council and housing systems.\n- **SQL** is not a general language but the one used to ask databases questions.\n- **C and C++** are close to the machine, used where raw speed matters, such as operating systems and games.\n\n::: remember\nChoosing a language is rarely a question of "best". It is about the team\'s skills, the ecosystem of ready-made tools, hiring, and how long the thing needs to be maintained. A very good answer to "why did you choose this language?" is about **people and maintenance**, not fashion.\n:::\n\n## Two ways to get from words to action\n\nSomeone has to translate the readable code into machine instructions. There are two broad approaches.\n\n![Compiling translates everything first and then runs; interpreting reads and runs as it goes](local:compile-vs-interpret.svg)\n\n**Compiled.** A **compiler** translates the whole program into a ready-to-run file *before* anyone uses it. It is like translating an entire book into French, printing it, then handing out the copies. Running is fast, and many mistakes are caught up front, but there is a build step. Examples: C, C++, Go, Rust, and (in a slightly different form) Java and C#.\n\n**Interpreted.** An **interpreter** reads the code and carries it out as it goes. It is like having a live interpreter at a meeting, translating sentence by sentence. There is no build step so changes are quick to try, but it is usually slower and some mistakes only appear when that line is reached. Examples: Python, PHP, Ruby and JavaScript (although modern JavaScript engines use clever tricks to speed up).\n\nIn reality the line is blurry. Many languages mix both, compiling to an in-between form just before running. You do not need to remember which is which. You need to know that **the choice affects speed, how mistakes are found, and how software is built and released**.\n\n## Why this matters to the Repairs project\n\n- **"It works on my machine."** Software that depends on a particular language version or setting can behave differently elsewhere. This is one reason for the **environments** you met in the first course, and for containers later in this one.\n- **Build steps are part of release.** A compiled system needs to be built before release, which is why release pipelines (CI/CD) matter.\n- **Hiring and support.** A system written in a rare language can be hard to maintain when its authors leave. Ask suppliers which language and framework they use and how common the skill is.\n- **Types catch mistakes early.** Some languages require you to say in advance what kind of thing each value is (text, number, date). These "typed" languages catch an entire class of mistakes before release, at the cost of a bit more writing. Many teams now add types even to JavaScript (via TypeScript).\n\n::: try\nOpen any web page, press **F12** (or right-click and choose Inspect) and open the **Console** tab. Type `2 + 2` and press Enter. You have just used an **interpreter**: the browser read your line and ran it immediately. Now type `"Repair " + 4821` and press Enter. Notice how it joined text and a number without complaint. Many languages would refuse; JavaScript quietly guesses. That flexibility is a strength and also a source of surprising bugs.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nImagine a supplier says the repairs platform is "written in a niche language only we use". Write down two questions about risk to the Repairs project, for instance what happens to maintenance if the supplier is bought or closes.\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_questions (lesson_id, kind, format, prompt, sort_order)
