INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'The DOM: how a browser builds a living page', 'How HTML becomes a tree the browser can change, and what that means for forms, accessibility and "why did the page change?".', '## From text to tree\n\nIn the first course you saw that a browser downloads HTML, CSS and JavaScript. Here is the next level of detail. When the browser receives HTML, it does not just paint it on the screen. It reads it and builds a **tree** of objects in memory called the **DOM**: the **Document Object Model**.\n\n![HTML text is parsed into a tree; JavaScript can change a branch and the page updates](local:dom-tree.svg)\n\nEach element on the page (a heading, a paragraph, a button) is a **node** in the tree. Elements inside other elements become **children**. The whole page is a family tree with the document at the top.\n\n## Why the tree matters\n\nBecause the page is a tree of live objects, not just text, **JavaScript can change it while you watch**:\n\n- Change the words in a heading.\n- Add a new row to a table without reloading the page.\n- Hide a section, show an error message, switch a button on or off.\n- React to what you do: clicks, typing, scrolling.\n\nThat is how a page can update a repair\'s status, validate a postcode as you type, or reveal extra questions on a form, all without a trip back to the server for a brand new page. When you hear **"single-page application"**, it means a page that mostly rebuilds parts of its own DOM rather than loading new pages.\n\n## Two views of the same page\n\nThis explains something that confuses many people. There are two things you can look at in a browser:\n\n- **View source** shows the original HTML text the server sent.\n- **Inspect (Elements)** shows the **current DOM**, including every change JavaScript has made since.\n\nOn a simple page they look alike. On a modern app, the source may be nearly empty (a single placeholder) and the DOM is full of content built by JavaScript.\n\n## Accessibility lives in the DOM\n\nAssistive technology, such as a screen reader, does not look at pixels. It reads the DOM (and a related structure called the **accessibility tree**). That is why using the right element matters. A real `<button>` is automatically focusable by keyboard and announced as "button". A decorated `<div>` made to look like a button is invisible to a screen reader unless extra work is done. Good semantic HTML is accessibility for free.\n\n::: try\nOpen any web page. Right-click a heading and choose **Inspect**. In the Elements panel, double-click the heading text and type something new, then press Enter. The page changes instantly, only in your browser, and reloading undoes it. You have just edited the DOM. (Nothing on the real website was changed, which is the point: the DOM is a copy living in your browser.) Now compare with **View page source** (right-click, View page source) to see the original.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nThe DOM is the browser\'s live copy of the page. JavaScript edits the copy, the screen follows. The server\'s original is untouched.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nA repair form hides the "access instructions" box until the tenant ticks "Someone else will let the contractor in". Which two things in this lesson explain how that works, and what must a developer get right so a screen reader user also hears the new box appear?\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
