INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Three ways of thinking: procedural, object-oriented and functional', 'The main styles developers use to organise code, explained with repairs examples and no code needed.', '## Same job, different mindset\n\nYou will sometimes hear developers argue about "OOP versus functional". It sounds like a religion. It is better understood as three **ways of organising the same work**, like three ways to run a kitchen. None is universally best, and most real systems mix them.\n\n![The same job done in a procedural, object-oriented and functional style](local:three-ways-of-thinking.svg)\n\n## Procedural: a recipe\n\n**Procedural** code is a list of steps carried out in order, with shared information kept in the background. "Take the repair, check its priority, set the target date, save it." It is the oldest and simplest style and works very well for short scripts and small jobs. It becomes harder to manage when the recipe grows to thousands of steps all touching the same shared information.\n\n## Object-oriented (OOP): self-contained things\n\n**Object-oriented programming** organises code around **objects**: self-contained things that bundle together *information* and the *actions* that can be done to it. A **Repair** object knows its address, priority and status, and knows how to `escalate()` or `close()` itself. Other parts of the system simply ask it to, without needing to know how.\n\nThe ideas you will hear:\n\n- **Class:** the blueprint for a kind of object (the idea of a Repair).\n- **Object / instance:** one actual thing built from it (Repair 4821).\n- **Encapsulation:** keeping the insides private, so only the object changes its own information.\n- **Inheritance:** a specialised kind of thing can build on a general one (an Emergency Repair is a Repair with extra rules).\n\nIt maps naturally onto real-world things, which is why it dominates business software (Java, C#, and much of PHP and Python). Done badly it creates deep tangles of objects depending on other objects.\n\n## Functional: machines on a production line\n\n**Functional programming** builds a system out of small **functions** that take something in and give something out, **without changing anything else**. "Given a list of repairs, return only the urgent ones. Given those, return them sorted by date." Each step is a self-contained machine; they are chained together.\n\nBecause a function never secretly changes anything outside itself, it is easy to **test** (same input always gives the same output) and safe to run **at the same time** as other work, which helps with the concurrency problems you will meet later in this course. Much modern JavaScript, and tools like React, borrow heavily from this style.\n\n## So which is "right"?\n\nNeither. Teams choose based on the problem and what suits the people. What matters to you:\n\n- **Consistency.** A codebase that follows one clear style is easier to maintain than one that mixes everything randomly.\n- **The vocabulary.** If you hear "we model each repair as an object" or "these are pure functions", you now know it is about how the code is organised, not what the service does.\n- **No style fixes a poor design.** A tidy structure around the wrong business rules is still wrong.\n\n::: try\nUse a spreadsheet to feel the difference. A **functional** approach: a column with a formula that takes another column\'s value and produces a result, never altering the original. A **procedural/stateful** approach: a macro that goes through the sheet changing cells in place. Which is easier to trust and check? Most people find the formula easier, because nothing else was touched. That intuition is the heart of functional thinking.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nThe Repairs project has a rule: "An emergency that is not attended within 4 hours must be escalated." Describe in one sentence how that rule might live inside a Repair object (OOP), and in one sentence how it might be a function that checks a list of repairs (functional). Notice that the **rule is identical**, only the organisation differs.\n:::', 3, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
