INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'WordPress, CMSs and PHP: how much of the web is built', 'What a content management system is, why so many sites use one, and where the risks are.', '## Websites without writing code\n\nMost websites are not built by hand from scratch. They are created in a **Content Management System (CMS)**: software that lets people who are not developers create, edit and publish pages through a friendly screen, while the system takes care of storing the content and displaying it consistently.\n\nThe best-known is **WordPress**, which runs roughly **four in ten websites on the internet**. Others include Drupal, Joomla and many commercial platforms used by councils, plus "headless" CMSs that supply content to other systems via an API.\n\n## What is inside a CMS\n\nA CMS is a good example of the layers from the first course working together:\n\n- **Back end in PHP.** WordPress is written in **PHP**, a language that runs on the server and builds each page when requested. (This learning site is also PHP.)\n- **Database.** Page text, menus and settings are stored in a **MySQL** database.\n- **Theme.** Controls the look. Change the theme and the whole site looks different, without touching content.\n- **Plugins.** Add-on packages giving extra abilities: forms, SEO, booking, payments. There are tens of thousands.\n- **Editor.** The part you see, where editors type and click.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- Editors can work without developers, so changes are fast.\n- A huge ecosystem and many skilled people.\n- Content is separated from design, so a redesign does not mean retyping.\n\n## Risks to watch\n\n- **Plugin sprawl.** Every plugin is code from someone else. Out-of-date or abandoned plugins are the most common way WordPress sites get hacked.\n- **Updates.** The CMS, themes and plugins all need regular updating and testing.\n- **Performance and accessibility.** A theme or plugin can quietly make a site slow or inaccessible. Do not assume a CMS is accessible; test it.\n- **Content sprawl.** Without governance, old and duplicate pages pile up.\n\n## CMS versus an API-first system\n\nA CMS is excellent for pages that people write: news, guidance, "how to report a repair". A repairs *transaction* (a job with a status, an appointment and a price) belongs in a proper line-of-business system with an API, not as pages in a CMS. Many good services combine both: content from the CMS, live data from the API.\n\n::: try\nCheck what a site runs on. In your browser, open a page, right-click and choose **View page source**, then search (Ctrl/Cmd+F) for `wp-content`. If it appears, the site uses WordPress. Try this on three council websites or other organisations you know. Then try adding `/wp-login.php` to a WordPress site\'s address to see the standard login page that attackers also know about, which is why good sites protect it.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nA CMS is for **content people write**. A line-of-business system with an API is for **transactions the organisation processes**. Mixing them up is a classic and expensive mistake.\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
