INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Reuse, standards and accessibility: build once, build well', 'Why the best teams reuse patterns, how standards save money, and why accessibility is built in rather than bolted on.', '## The reuse ladder\n\nEvery time a team builds the same thing from scratch, money is wasted and inconsistency creeps in. Mature teams climb a **ladder of reuse**: the higher you go, the more is shared and the less is rebuilt.\n\n![The ladder of reuse from copy and paste up to a shared platform](local:reuse-ladder.svg)\n\n1. **Copy and paste.** Quick, but now there are two versions to fix. The most common and least efficient.\n2. **Functions and components.** One piece of code used in many places inside a project.\n3. **Shared libraries and a design system.** Components used across many services: buttons, forms, address lookups, with the design and accessibility baked in. (You met design systems in the first course.)\n4. **Patterns and standards.** Agreed, repeatable ways to solve common problems, such as "how we ask for an address" or "how a service reports an error".\n5. **Shared platform.** Common services (login, notifications, payments, address lookup) that every service uses instead of building its own.\n\n## Patterns: known good answers\n\nA **design pattern** is a proven, named solution to a recurring problem. The same patterns appear again and again: *"Check your answers" pages, address lookup, appointment booking, error summaries*. In code, patterns have names such as **queue**, **adapter** or **observer**. Naming them lets teams talk quickly and avoid reinventing.\n\n## Standards save money\n\nStandards are agreed rules that let different people\'s work fit together:\n\n- **Web standards** (HTML, CSS, HTTP) are why any browser can open any site.\n- **API standards** (REST, OpenAPI, JSON) mean systems from different suppliers can talk.\n- **Data standards** (such as the UPRN for properties) mean records from different systems match.\n- **Accessibility standards** (WCAG) define what "usable by everyone" means.\n\nA public body that writes standards into its procurement gets systems that fit together. One that does not ends up with a collection of one-off islands.\n\n## Accessibility (a11y): built in, not bolted on\n\n**Accessibility** means everyone can use the service, including people who are blind, have low vision, are deaf, have motor difficulties, dyslexia or cognitive impairments, or are simply on a small screen in bright sun. The shorthand **a11y** means "a, then 11 letters, then y".\n\nFor UK public sector bodies it is also a **legal duty**: websites and apps must meet **WCAG 2.2 level AA** and publish an **accessibility statement**.\n\nWhat it looks like in practice:\n\n- Real headings, buttons and labels (from the DOM lesson) so a screen reader can understand the page.\n- Everything usable by **keyboard alone**, with a visible focus marker.\n- Enough **colour contrast**, and never using colour alone to carry meaning.\n- Text alternatives (alt text) for meaningful images.\n- Clear language and clear errors.\n\nThe big saving: it is **far cheaper to build accessibly from the start than to repair later**, and the same work helps everyone (captions help in noisy rooms, clear forms help in a hurry). This is also where reuse pays off: fix the accessibility of a shared component once, and every service improves.\n\n::: try\nPut your mouse aside and use **only the keyboard** on a council web form: Tab to move forward, Shift+Tab backwards, Enter or Space to press. Can you reach every field? Can you always see where you are? Can you submit? Then press **Cmd+F5** (Mac VoiceOver) or search for "Narrator" on Windows, and listen to the first screen. Ten minutes of this teaches more about accessibility than any report.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nFor the Repairs project, name one shared component (for instance an address lookup or a photo upload) that every part of the service should use rather than rebuild. What would go wrong if three suppliers each built their own?\n:::', 3, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
