INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Git, GitHub and code review: how changes are tracked and checked', 'Version control, branches, pull requests and why a second pair of eyes is the cheapest safety net in software.', '## The problem: many people, one codebase\n\nImagine several people editing the same document at once, with no "track changes" and no history. Chaos. Software teams face this daily, so they use **version control**: a system that records every change, who made it, when and why, and lets work be combined or undone safely.\n\nThe standard tool is **Git**. **GitHub** (and similar services such as GitLab and Azure DevOps) is a website that hosts Git projects and adds teamwork features.\n\n## The vocabulary\n\n- **Repository (repo):** the project folder plus its complete history.\n- **Commit:** a saved step, with a short message saying what changed and why. Like a labelled snapshot.\n- **Branch:** a safe, separate line of work. Develop a new feature without disturbing the stable version.\n- **Merge:** combine a branch back into the main one.\n- **Pull request (PR):** a proposal to merge a branch, which colleagues review before it goes in.\n- **Main:** the trusted branch that reflects what is, or is about to be, live.\n\n![A feature branch is created, committed to, reviewed through a pull request and merged into main](local:git-flow.svg)\n\n## Code review: the cheapest safety net\n\nBefore changes join the main version, at least one other person **reads them**. Reviewers look for:\n\n- **Mistakes** and missed cases.\n- **Security** problems, such as a secret key accidentally left in the code.\n- **Clarity**: will someone understand this in a year?\n- **Fit**: does it follow the team\'s patterns and standards?\n\nReviews catch problems far more cheaply than users do, and they spread knowledge so no single person is the only one who understands a part. Many teams also set rules so that code cannot be merged until it has been approved **and** automated tests have passed.\n\n## Why you should care\n\n- **A full history is an audit trail.** Every change can be traced and, if needed, reversed. That is valuable for a public body.\n- **Ask who can see the code.** If a supplier writes software for the council, does the council have access to the repository? Owning a system without access to its code is a lock-in risk.\n- **Secrets.** Passwords and keys must **never** be stored in a repository. Public repositories are scanned by attackers within minutes.\n- **Issues and boards.** GitHub also tracks bugs and requests (called **issues**), which gives non-developers a way to see progress and join in.\n\n::: try\nVisit **github.com** and search for a project you have heard of, for instance `alphagov/govuk-frontend` (the UK government\'s design system code). Open it and look at: the **Commits** list (history), the **Pull requests** tab (proposed changes and the discussion on them) and the **Issues** tab. Open one closed pull request and read the review conversation. You are watching code review happen in public.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nThe Repairs project uses suppliers\' systems. Write two requests you would make about code access and history, for example "the council holds a copy of the source code and release history in an escrow arrangement".\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
