INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'DevOps: building it and running it as one team', 'The idea behind DevOps, the pipeline from idea to live, and the habits that make frequent, safe releases possible.', '## The old wall\n\nTraditionally, one group (**development**) wrote software and "threw it over the wall" to another (**operations**) who had to keep it running. Developers wanted to ship change; operations wanted stability. They had opposing goals and blamed each other when things went wrong. Releases were big, rare and frightening.\n\n**DevOps** (Development + Operations) tears down the wall. It is a way of working in which the people who build and the people who run a service are **one team**, share responsibility for it, and use **automation** so that releases become small, frequent and boring.\n\n## The pipeline\n\nThe heart of DevOps is an automated **pipeline** that takes a change from a developer\'s keyboard to live, with checks along the way. You met CI/CD in the first course; here is the full picture:\n\n1. **Plan:** a need is captured as a task.\n2. **Code:** the change is written and reviewed.\n3. **Build:** the software is assembled automatically.\n4. **Test:** automated tests run (and people test too).\n5. **Release:** the change is packaged and moved to the live environment.\n6. **Deploy:** it goes live, ideally in a way that can be reversed.\n7. **Operate and monitor:** the team watches how it behaves.\n8. **Learn:** what the monitoring and users say feeds the next plan.\n\nIt is a **loop**, not a line.\n\n## The habits behind it\n\n- **Small changes, often.** A small change is easy to understand, test and undo. A giant release is a gamble.\n- **Automate the repetitive.** Anything done by hand repeatedly (building, testing, deploying) is a chance for human error.\n- **Infrastructure as code.** Servers and settings are described in files (often **YAML**, which you will meet soon) rather than set up by clicking, so environments are identical and can be rebuilt at will.\n- **Monitoring and alerts.** The team knows something is wrong before users complain.\n- **Blameless post-mortems.** After an incident, the team studies *how the system allowed it*, not *whom to blame*. This is how organisations really improve.\n- **Feature flags.** New features are shipped switched off, then enabled for a few users, then everyone. Problems can be undone by flipping a switch.\n\n## DevOps and security\n\nYou will also hear **DevSecOps**: building security checks into the pipeline (scanning for known vulnerable components, detecting secrets in code) so that security is continuous, not an annual inspection.\n\n## Four measures worth knowing\n\nResearchers who studied thousands of teams found four simple measures separate high performers from the rest:\n\n- **Deployment frequency:** how often changes go live.\n- **Lead time:** how long from an idea to live.\n- **Change failure rate:** how often a release causes a problem.\n- **Time to restore:** how quickly service returns after a failure.\n\nNotice the surprise: the best teams are *both* faster *and* more stable. Speed and safety are not opposites when automation is good.\n\n::: try\nLook at the public **status page** of a service you rely on (for example `githubstatus.com`) and read some past incident reports. Look for the language of DevOps: timeline, root cause, what we are changing. Compare with how a supplier of your own systems reports incidents. Is it open, specific and focused on learning?\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nAsk a supplier: "How often do you release, how long does a change take from request to live, and how do you roll back?" Write down what answers would suggest a mature team and what answers would worry you.\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
