INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Operating systems and browsers: why the same thing looks different', 'The layers that sit between software and hardware, and why testing across devices is not optional.', '## What an operating system does\n\nAn **operating system (OS)** is the core software that manages a device\'s hardware and lets other programs run on it. Without it you would have to write instructions for the screen, keyboard, memory and storage in every program. The main families:\n\n- **Windows:** dominant on council desktops.\n- **macOS:** Apple laptops and desktops.\n- **Linux:** the quiet giant. It runs most servers, most cloud services, Android\'s core and a great deal of the internet.\n- **iOS and Android:** the two phone and tablet systems. Together they carry most tenants\' contact with the council.\n\nPrograms are usually written for a particular OS. An app built for Windows will not simply run on a phone. This is why developers produce different versions (**native apps**) or build things for the web, which works everywhere there is a browser.\n\n## Browsers and rendering engines\n\nA **browser** is itself a program that runs on the OS, and its core is a **rendering engine** that turns HTML, CSS and JavaScript into the page. There are only three major engines today:\n\n- **Blink:** used by Chrome, Edge, Opera, Brave and others.\n- **WebKit:** used by Safari. **On iPhones and iPads, all browsers must use WebKit under the hood**, so even "Chrome on iPhone" is really Safari\'s engine in disguise (a rule that is being challenged in some regions).\n- **Gecko:** used by Firefox.\n\nBecause the engines are separate programs written by different teams, they can interpret the standards slightly differently. Usually the result is identical. Occasionally a feature works in one and not another, or a layout shifts by a few pixels.\n\n## Why this affects the Repairs project\n\n- **People use what they have.** Tenants arrive on old phones, small screens, slow connections, shared family tablets and public library computers. Staff may use locked-down desktop browsers that are several versions behind.\n- **Test the real mix.** Check analytics to see which browsers and devices your users actually use, then test on those, not just on a developer\'s new laptop.\n- **Browser support policy.** Agree which browsers and versions the service must work on, and write it into contracts. GOV.UK, for example, publishes its own.\n- **Progressive enhancement.** Build so that the basics work on anything (a plain form that submits), then add improvements for modern browsers. If the fancy JavaScript fails, the tenant can still report the leak.\n- **Operating systems need updates.** Old, unsupported systems (for instance Windows versions that no longer get security patches) are a security risk and worth tracking on risk registers.\n\n## Mobile first\n\nMore people use phones than desktops for most public services. "Mobile first" means designing for the small screen and slow connection first, then expanding for larger screens. This tends to produce clearer, simpler services for everybody.\n\n::: try\nIn Chrome or Edge, press **F12**, then click the small **device toolbar** icon (a phone and tablet). Pick "iPhone" or "Galaxy" from the list and reload a council page. Switch on **network throttling** (in the Network tab, choose "Slow 3G") and reload again. This is closer to what many tenants experience. How long does it take to become usable?\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nList five devices and browsers a tenant of social housing might realistically use to report a repair. Which of them would a developer\'s test set most easily forget?\n:::', 4, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
