INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Database types: choosing the right tool for the data', 'Relational, document, key-value, graph and time-series databases, with what each is best at.', '## One size does not fit all\n\nIn the first course you met the **relational database**: tables of rows and columns linked by keys, queried with SQL. It is the workhorse for most business systems and for good reason. But it is not the only design. Over the last twenty years, other kinds were created for jobs the table does less well. Knowing the families helps you understand suppliers\' choices.\n\n![Five database families side by side with the kind of job each is best at](local:database-types.svg)\n\n## The main families\n\n**Relational (SQL).** Data in tables with strict structure and relationships between them. Best for: transactions, records that must be accurate and consistent, anything you want to count, join and report on. Examples: **MySQL, PostgreSQL, Microsoft SQL Server, Oracle**. A repairs system\'s core records almost certainly live here.\n\n**Document.** Each record is a flexible, self-describing **document**, usually JSON, and records in the same collection can have different shapes. Best for: content, catalogues, data whose shape changes often or varies a lot between items. Examples: **MongoDB, Couchbase**. Think of a filing cabinet of folders, each holding whatever its owner put in.\n\n**Key-value.** The simplest design: store a value under a name (the **key**) and fetch it back by that name, extremely fast. Best for: sessions (the "who is logged in" information), caches, counters, queues. Examples: **Redis, Memcached**. Think of a cloakroom: hand in a ticket, get your coat.\n\n**Graph.** Stores things and the **relationships** between them as first-class parts, so questions about connections are quick: "who is connected to whom, through what, within three steps?" Best for: networks, dependency maps, recommendations, fraud detection. Examples: **Neo4j, Amazon Neptune**. You will see more in the next lesson.\n\n**Time-series.** Built for values recorded over time, such as sensor readings, and for questions about trends and averages. Best for: monitoring, internet-of-things devices, energy and environmental data (for example damp and temperature sensors in homes). Examples: **InfluxDB, TimescaleDB**.\n\nThere are also **search engines** (such as Elasticsearch) that make huge amounts of text searchable instantly, and **data warehouses** (from the first course) built for analysis rather than day-to-day transactions.\n\n## "NoSQL"\n\nYou will hear **NoSQL** used for anything that is not a traditional relational database. It is a label for what they are *not*, and mixes very different designs. It is more useful to ask which family is meant.\n\n## Choosing: the questions to ask\n\n- **How important is strict accuracy?** If money, legal records or safety are involved, a relational database with transactions (the next module) is normally the right core.\n- **How regular is the data?** Fixed, well-understood shapes suit tables; wildly varied shapes suit documents.\n- **What will you ask of it?** Many connected questions suit graphs; "latest value for this key" suits key-value; trends over time suit time-series.\n- **How fast and how big?** Some designs shine at enormous scale or speed, but at the cost of strict guarantees.\n\nReal systems often use **several**: a relational database as the trusted record, a key-value store for speed, a search engine for finding things, and a warehouse for reporting. This is called **polyglot persistence**, which simply means "using the right store for each job".\n\n::: try\nSearch the web for an online "MongoDB playground" and paste in `{"address": "12 High St", "priority": "urgent", "photos": [1, 2]}`, then a second document with different fields. Notice the database accepts both. Then think how a relational table would handle a repair that suddenly needed an extra "scaffolding" field.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nThe core record of a repair is best in a relational database. The supporting jobs (speed, search, sensors, connections) are often best handled by other kinds. Neither is "better" overall.\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
