INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Concurrency and race conditions: the bugs you cannot see', 'What happens when two people act at the same moment, why testing often misses it, and how to spot the risk.', '## It worked perfectly in testing\n\nSome of the most damaging software faults never show up when one person tests one thing at a time. They appear only when **many things happen at once**, which is exactly what a busy live service does. This is **concurrency**: several users, processes or systems acting at the same time.\n\n## The race condition\n\nA **race condition** is a bug where the outcome depends on *which action happens to finish first*. Here is the classic story, with a repairs flavour: one slot left for an appointment.\n\n![Two planners both read one slot left, both book it, and the slot is double-booked](local:race-condition.svg)\n\n1. Planner A looks: **1 appointment slot left**.\n2. Planner B looks a split second later: **1 slot left**.\n3. A books it and the system writes "slot taken, 0 left".\n4. B, who still believes there is a slot, also books it and writes "0 left".\n\nResult: **two bookings, one slot**. Neither planner did anything wrong. Neither step looked wrong on its own. The fault is in the *gap* between "check" and "act". Two operatives turn up, or a tenant is told two different things.\n\nThe same pattern causes:\n\n- **Lost updates:** two people edit the same repair and the second save silently overwrites the first (a danger with the whole-record **PUT** you met earlier).\n- **Duplicate records** from a double-clicked submit button.\n- **Wrong totals** when two payments update a balance together.\n- **Duplicate messages** when a request is retried.\n\n## Why it is so hard to find\n\nRace conditions are **intermittent**. The timing must be just wrong, so they may occur once in a thousand uses. They often vanish when you try to reproduce them. The result is the dread phrase "we can\'t reproduce it". They tend to appear only under real load, such as the morning after a storm when thousands report at once.\n\n## How teams defend against them\n\nYou will recognise these ideas in good suppliers\' answers:\n\n- **Locking:** reserve the data so only one process can change it at a time while others wait. Simple and safe, but can slow things down.\n- **Optimistic concurrency (version checks):** each record carries a version number. When you save, you say "update this *if it is still version 7*". If someone else changed it, your save is refused and you are shown the conflict instead of silently overwriting. The HTTP form of this is the **ETag** and **If-Match** header.\n- **Atomic operations:** do the check and the change as one indivisible step ("book if available").\n- **Idempotency keys:** a unique label sent with a request so that a repeat of the same request is recognised and done only once (the next lesson).\n- **Unique constraints:** the database itself refuses a second booking for the same slot.\n\n::: try\nOpen the same web page that has a form (the learning site\'s comment box, or any online editor) in **two browser tabs**. Click Save/Submit in both at nearly the same time, or edit the same item in both tabs and save the second after the first. Does the second silently win? Does it warn you? You are testing for a lost update. Notice that most simple systems do not protect you.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nRace conditions live in the **gap between checking and doing**. The defences are: make the check-and-act one step, make the system notice a conflict, or make repeating a request harmless.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nList three places in the Repairs service where two people or two systems might change the same thing at the same moment (appointment slots, a job\'s status updated by both a contractor\'s app and a planner, a payment). For each, what would the tenant or the council experience if the second change overwrote the first?\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
