INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'AI: where it came from and how to get the best from it', 'What modern AI tools are, what they are good and bad at, and a practical approach for using them safely at work.', '## A short history\n\n"Artificial intelligence" has been a research field since the 1950s, with periods of excitement followed by disappointment. Three changes brought it into everyday life:\n\n- **Data:** the internet created vast amounts of text, images and examples to learn from.\n- **Computing power:** specialised chips made it feasible to train enormous models.\n- **Better methods:** especially a design called the **transformer** (2017), which led to **large language models (LLMs)**, the technology behind tools like ChatGPT and Claude.\n\nFrom late 2022, conversational AI became available to anyone with a browser, and the pace of change has been extraordinary.\n\n## What it actually does\n\nA **large language model** is trained on huge amounts of text to **predict what words are likely to come next**. From that single skill emerge abilities to summarise, draft, translate, explain, write code and answer questions. It does not look things up in a database of facts unless it is given tools to do so. It generates plausible language.\n\nThat explains both its power and its biggest weakness.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **First drafts and rewriting:** letters, summaries, plain-English versions of complex text.\n- **Explaining and learning:** "explain this error message" or "what does this contract clause mean?"\n- **Structuring and sorting:** turning messy notes or free-text repair descriptions into categories.\n- **Coding help:** writing, explaining and testing code, which is making developers more productive.\n- **Drudge work:** reformatting, extracting information from documents, transcribing meetings.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n- **Hallucination.** A model can state something false with total confidence, inventing facts, references or figures. It does not know when it is wrong.\n- **Out of date or unaware of you.** It knows nothing about your internal rules unless you give it the information.\n- **Inconsistency.** Ask twice, get different answers.\n- **Bias.** It reflects patterns in its training data, including unfair ones.\n- **Privacy and security.** Anything typed into a public tool may leave your control. Personal data and confidential information must follow your organisation\'s policy and UK GDPR.\n\n## The loop that works\n\nThe most reliable way to use AI is as a fast, tireless **assistant**, not as an authority, with a person always in the loop.\n\n![The loop: give clear context, get a draft, check it against facts, and refine](local:ai-loop.svg)\n\n1. **Give context.** Say what you want, for whom, what to use, and what good looks like. Clear **prompts** produce much better results.\n2. **Get a draft.** Treat it as a draft by a quick, keen junior colleague.\n3. **Check.** Verify facts, numbers and references against reliable sources. The more it matters, the more you check.\n4. **Refine.** Ask for changes, point out mistakes, iterate.\n\n### A prompt that works\n\nCompare "Write about repairs" with:\n\n> You are helping a council housing team. Summarise the following repair complaint in three bullet points for a manager, in plain English, and list any safety risks. Do not guess missing details. Complaint: ...\n\nThe second states role, task, audience, format and a rule against inventing. That is most of the skill.\n\n## Using it responsibly at work\n\n- **Know the policy.** Use only tools your organisation approves, and never paste personal or confidential data into unapproved ones.\n- **Keep a human accountable.** Decisions affecting residents, such as who gets a repair first, need a person who can explain and own them.\n- **Be transparent.** Where AI has helped produce something public, follow your organisation\'s guidance on saying so.\n- **Test for fairness.** If AI helps prioritise or triage, check outcomes across different groups of tenants.\n- **Start small and measure.** Choose a low-risk task, measure time saved and errors, then widen.\n\n::: try\nPick a real, non-confidential paragraph of dense text, such as published guidance. Ask an approved AI tool: "Rewrite this in plain English for a tenant, at a reading age of 12." Then **check it**: did it change any meaning, drop a condition or invent a detail? Now ask it a question you know the answer to but is slightly obscure, and see whether it admits uncertainty or bluffs. You have just met both its usefulness and its hallucinations.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nList two Repairs project tasks where AI could save real time at low risk (for example summarising long repair notes for planners), and two where you would not allow it to act without a human decision (for example deciding priority for a vulnerable household). Explain the line you drew.\n:::', 3, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
